<section class="section reveal" id="what-is-series">
  <span class="section-label">Fundamentals</span>
  <h2>Understanding Pandas Series</h2>

  <p>A Series is a one-dimensional labeled array — think of it as a single column from a DataFrame or a Python list with labels (an index). Every Series has two components: values (the actual data) and an index (the labels for each value). Unlike a list, a Series knows what each element represents because every value has a corresponding label.</p>

  <p>In practice, a Series is what you get when you extract a single column from a DataFrame. But you can also create Series independently for various data manipulation tasks. Series are the building blocks of DataFrames — a DataFrame is essentially a dictionary of Series with the same index.</p>

  <div class="analogy">
    <h4>💡 Think of It This Way</h4>
    <p>If a DataFrame is a spreadsheet, then a Series is a single column from that spreadsheet. The column has a name (the Series.name property) and each cell has a label (the index). You can access values by their label just like looking up a word in a dictionary, or by their position like accessing a list element.</p>
  </div>

