<section class="section reveal" id="advanced-indexing">
  <span class="section-label">Core Concepts</span>
  <h2>Advanced Indexing with .loc and .iloc</h2>

  <p>Pandas provides two complementary indexing systems: .loc (label-based) and .iloc (position-based). Mastering both is essential for efficient data manipulation. Label-based indexing (.loc) is more readable and less error-prone when you have meaningful index names. Position-based indexing (.iloc) is perfect for iteration and when you need the first 10 rows regardless of their labels.</p>

  <div class="infographic reveal">
    <div class="infographic-title">Indexing Methods Compared</div>
    <div class="compare-grid">
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>df.loc (Labels)</h5>
        <ul>
          <li>Access by index label</li>
          <li>Most explicit and readable</li>
          <li>Inclusive on both ends</li>
          <li>Works with boolean arrays</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>df.iloc (Positions)</h5>
        <ul>
          <li>Access by integer position</li>
          <li>Like list slicing</li>
          <li>Excludes end index</li>
          <li>Standard Python slicing rules</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>df.at (Scalar Label)</h5>
        <ul>
          <li>Single scalar by label</li>
          <li>Fastest for single values</li>
          <li>df.at[row, col]</li>
          <li>Returns scalar, not Series</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>df.iat (Scalar Position)</h5>
        <ul>
          <li>Single scalar by position</li>
          <li>Fastest positional access</li>
          <li>df.iat[row, col]</li>
          <li>Returns scalar, not Series</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Create sample data</span>
<span class="va">df</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">pd</span>.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({
    <span class="st">"A"</span>: [<span class="nm">10</span>, <span class="nm">20</span>, <span class="nm">30</span>],
    <span class="st">"B"</span>: [<span class="nm">40</span>, <span class="nm">50</span>, <span class="nm">60</span>]
}, <span class="va">index</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="st">"x"</span>, <span class="st">"y"</span>, <span class="st">"z"</span>])

<span class="cm"># .loc with labels</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">loc</span>[<span class="st">"x"</span>])           <span class="cm"># Row 'x'</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">loc</span>[<span class="st">"x"</span>:<span class="st">"y"</span>, <span class="st">"A"</span>])  <span class="cm"># Rows x:y (inclusive!), col A</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">loc</span>[<span class="st">"x"</span>, <span class="st">"A"</span>])      <span class="cm"># Single scalar: 10</span>

<span class="cm"># .iloc with positions</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">iloc</span>[<span class="nm">0</span>])            <span class="cm"># First row</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">iloc</span>[<span class="nm">0</span>:<span class="nm">2</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>])   <span class="cm"># Rows 0:2 (excl 2), col 0</span>

<span class="cm"># Scalar access (fastest)</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">at</span>[<span class="st">"x"</span>, <span class="st">"A"</span>])       <span class="cm"># Label-based: 10</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">iat</span>[<span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>])        <span class="cm"># Position-based: 10</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> .loc uses labels and is inclusive on both ends (slicing "x":"y" includes both). .iloc uses positions and excludes the end (like list slicing). .at and .iat access single scalar values and are significantly faster than returning a Series when you need just one value.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>.loc uses labels — most readable, inclusive on both ends</li>
      <li>.iloc uses positions — like list slicing, excludes end</li>
      <li>.at[label] and .iat[position] access single scalars efficiently</li>
      <li>Boolean indexing works with .loc for conditional selection</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="section-divider">

<section class="section reveal" id="boolean-indexing">
  <span class="section-label">Practical Skills</span>
  <h2>Boolean Indexing and Conditional Selection</h2>

  <p>One of the most powerful features of Pandas is boolean indexing — filtering rows based on conditions. Instead of looping through rows and testing each one, you write a condition that creates a boolean Series, then use it to filter. This is both more readable and dramatically faster than loops.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Boolean indexing for filtering</span>
<span class="va">df</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">pd</span>.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({
    <span class="st">"Name"</span>: [<span class="st">"Alice"</span>, <span class="st">"Bob"</span>, <span class="st">"Charlie"</span>],
    <span class="st">"Age"</span>: [<span class="nm">25</span>, <span class="nm">35</span>, <span class="nm">28</span>],
    <span class="st">"Salary"</span>: [<span class="nm">60000</span>, <span class="nm">95000</span>, <span class="nm">70000</span>]
})

<span class="cm"># Create a boolean mask</span>
<span class="va">mask</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">"Age"</span>] <span class="op">></span> <span class="nm">26</span>

<span class="cm"># Use the mask to filter rows</span>
<span class="va">senior</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="va">mask</span>]        <span class="cm"># Rows where age > 26</span>

<span class="cm"># Combine conditions with & (AND), | (OR)</span>
<span class="va">rich_and_old</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[
    (<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">"Age"</span>] <span class="op">></span> <span class="nm">26</span>) <span class="op">&</span> (<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">"Salary"</span>] <span class="op">></span> <span class="nm">65000</span>)
]</code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> Boolean indexing creates a Series of True/False values that matches the DataFrame's index. When you pass this to the DataFrame, it returns only rows where the condition is True. Use & for AND, | for OR, and ~ for NOT. Parentheses are required around each condition.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <h4>🌍 Why This Matters</h4>
    <p><strong>In Financial AI:</strong> Boolean indexing lets you instantly find all transactions above a threshold, all trading days with volatility exceeding limits, or all securities in a certain sector. This is the foundation of risk monitoring and portfolio analysis.</p>
    <p><strong>In Life Sciences AI:</strong> Filter patients by age and diagnosis, select test results outside normal ranges, or identify outliers in genomic data — all without writing loops.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Boolean indexing filters rows using conditions</li>
      <li>Combine conditions with & (AND), | (OR), ~ (NOT)</li>
      <li>Always use parentheses around each condition</li>
      <li>Much faster than loops for large DataFrames</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="section-divider">

<section class="section reveal" id="type-conversion">
  <span class="section-label">Advanced</span>
  <h2>Converting Between Series and DataFrames</h2>

  <p>You frequently need to convert between Series and DataFrames, or reshape data from one form to another. Pandas makes these conversions seamless and provides multiple ways to do them depending on your use case.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Convert Series to DataFrame</span>
<span class="va">s</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">pd</span>.<span class="fn">Series</span>([<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>], <span class="va">name</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"values"</span>)

<span class="cm"># Method 1: to_frame()</span>
<span class="va">df</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">s</span>.<span class="fn">to_frame</span>()

<span class="cm"># Method 2: Create DataFrame from dict of Series</span>
<span class="va">df</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">pd</span>.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({
    <span class="st">"col1"</span>: <span class="va">series1</span>,
    <span class="st">"col2"</span>: <span class="va">series2</span>
})

<span class="cm"># Convert DataFrame to Series (single column only)</span>
<span class="va">s</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">"column_name"</span>]

<span class="cm"># Stack multiple columns into a single Series</span>
<span class="va">stacked</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">stack</span>()      <span class="cm"># MultiIndex Series</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> Series.to_frame() creates a single-column DataFrame from a Series. You can also create a DataFrame from a dictionary of Series. Extracting a column returns a Series. The stack() method creates a MultiIndex Series (hierarchical index), useful for reshaping wide data into long format.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Use Series.to_frame() to convert a Series to a single-column DataFrame</li>
      <li>Extract columns from DataFrames to get Series</li>
      <li>Create DataFrames from dictionaries of Series with aligned indices</li>
      <li>Use .stack() and .unstack() to reshape data</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

